74 years ago hundreds of caribbean  migrants climbed aboard the empire wind  rush ready to start their new lives in  britain it was just after the second  world war and the country needed help so  people like alfred who had already  served in the raf took up their  invitations  i was here before  i went home and i first think i said  it's mama when i went to home i'm going  back  with no time let me help chase i told  her i was going back and  i had no reasons to come back  and here i am still here  enjoying it  tomorrow this year's windrush day he  joined this tour returning to where it  all began  here at tilbury darks is where the  empire wind rush first arrived 74 years  ago today  aboard where hundreds of migrants from  across the caribbean who'd left friends  families and their homes behind to come  and help rebuild britain  but even though it was in july it was  cold it was windy and it was very  different for what most of them had been  used to  but even though they were invited when  they arrived they weren't welcomed like  this  many experience racism discrimination  and 70 years later deportations  this is not for black people only it is  for the whole  of britain  to understand  to learn  something of the history of why we're  here how we came to be here  today a brand new statue was unveiled at  waterloo station  people like baroness benjamin who  arrived in london from trinidad in 1960  4 000 miles across the atlantic ocean we  landed at southampton docks and we got  onto that train from southampton to  waterloo station and there i am  standing on platform 19 waterloo station  very close to here with my suitcase with  my grip so this this monument represents  my story  a story not just of that generation but  for the direct descendants of all of  those who settled raised families and  redefined what it meant to be british  shimon freeman powell sky news
 

